session "DFS_Skeletons" in DFS = "Directed_Set_Graphs" +
  description \<open>Generic depth-first search skeletons.

    DFS_Skeleton is the bare skeleton: a stack-based search over a Pair_Graph_Specs adjacency map,
    parameterized by callbacks, with its termination argument and the invariants (seen/finished,
    the stack shape) proved once and for all. DFS_Skel_More duplicates it solely to add an on_push
    callback, applied to the freshly-pushed state in the descend step, and DFS_Skel_More_Refine
    moves the adjacency map into the state so a callback may prune it, proving the loop equivalent
    to the one that reads a fixed map.

    Kept as a light session of its own so that consumers needing only a DFS skeleton -- e.g. the
    directed-cycle detection in Directed_Cycle_DFS, or an external development such as a datalog
    foundedness check -- need not depend on Graph_Algorithms_Dev, which pulls in
    Verified_SAT_Based_AI_Planning, BFS and Bellman-Ford.\<close>
  theories
    DFS_Skeleton
    DFS_Skel_More
    DFS_Skel_More_Refine

session "Directed_Cycle_DFS" in Directed_Cycles = "DFS_Skeletons" +
  description \<open>Directed-cycle detection by depth-first search.

    DFS_DirCycle is the single-root directed-cycle detector on the DFS_Skeletons skeleton: a cycle
    exists below the root iff the search meets a back edge, i.e. an edge into a seen but unfinished
    vertex. Graph_Topological_Order gives the certificate view -- a finite directed graph has a
    topological numbering iff it has no directed cycle, which is the usual way to certify
    acyclicity, checkable in one pass over the edges.

    On top of that sits a whole-graph (linear) directed-cycle search, the directed counterpart of
    DFS_Cycles: an outer sweep over the vertices calling the detector once per remaining root, each
    call pre-seeded with the region earlier calls already finished, so the whole run is linear
    rather than one fresh DFS per vertex. It is developed as a chain of refinements, each level
    removing one set operation from a hot loop and each justified by an equivalence back to the
    level below it, so that the correctness argument is stated once against the simplest version and
    inherited by the fastest one. Every level ships an inner pre-seeded DFS (..._Aux) and an outer
    sweep that calls it once per remaining root.

      * Level 0 -- DFS_DirCycle_Linear{_Aux}, on DFS_Skeleton and DFS_DirCycle unchanged. The
        reference implementation: the back-edge test recomputes "seen - finished" at every skeleton
        step, and the outer loop recomputes "V - finished" at every iteration.
      * Level 1 -- DFS_DirCycle_Linear_Tracked{_Aux}, on DFS_Skel_More. The gray (on-stack) and
        unfinished sets become state components maintained incrementally, so both of level 0's
        differences disappear. DFS_DirCycle_Linear_Tracked_Equiv relates the two levels.
      * Level 2 -- DFS_DirCycle_Linear_Tracked_Aux_Refine and DFS_DirCycle_Linear_Tracked_Refine,
        on DFS_Skel_More_Refine, where the adjacency map itself moves into the state and on_push
        deletes the pushed vertex's in-edges (via a static reverse adjacency map fixed by the
        instance, so the deletions cost one pass over the edges in total). The remaining difference,
        "neighbourhood - seen" (evaluated twice per step), goes too: the loop reads a plain
        neighbourhood. The outer sweep threads that pruned map from one inner call to the next, so
        the pruning is done once over the whole run rather than once per root; its state record
        extends level 1's, and the equivalence back to level 1 is a plain equation under the record
        package's truncate, from which soundness and completeness transport. The inner search needs
        one assumption the vset ADT does not supply, sel_cong -- that sel is determined by the
        element set -- because the two levels hand sel vsets built by different operations; the
        outer sweep does not, since it selects from the very vset level 1 selects from.\<close>
  theories
    DFS_DirCycle
    Graph_Topological_Order
    DFS_DirCycle_Linear_Aux
    DFS_DirCycle_Linear
    DFS_DirCycle_Linear_Tracked_Aux
    DFS_DirCycle_Linear_Tracked
    DFS_DirCycle_Linear_Tracked_Equiv
    DFS_DirCycle_Linear_Tracked_Aux_Refine
    DFS_DirCycle_Linear_Tracked_Refine

